mott6:es-0020 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0020" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p488/b3" ;
    ev:boundingBox "50.98,142.28,278.33,155.9" ;
    ev:extractedText "The most common type of key for shafts up to 6 1"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-2 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx488 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

mott6:es-0021 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0021" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p488/b4" ;
    ev:boundingBox "50.97,143.86,293.89,479.9" ;
    ev:extractedText "2 in in diameter is the square key, as illustrated in Figure 11–1(b). The rectangular key, Figure 11–1(c), is recommended for larger shafts and is used for smaller shafts where the shorter height can be tolerated. Both the square and the rectangular keys are referred to as parallel keys because the top and bottom and the sides of the key are parallel. (See Internet sites 1 and 20.) Table 11–1 gives the preferred dimensions for parallel keys as a function of shaft diameter …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-2 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx488 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

mott6:es-0022 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0022" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p488/b5" ;
    ev:boundingBox "308.97,51.86,551.89,183.9" ;
    ev:extractedText "keyseat, respectively (refer Figure 12–7). In general practice, the keyseats and keys are left with essentially square ends and edges. But radiused keyseats and chamfered keys can be used to reduce the stress concentrations. Table 11–2 shows suggested values from ANSI Standard B17.1. As alternates to the use of parallel keys, taper keys, gib head keys, pin keys, and Woodruff keys can be used to provide special features of installation or operation. Figure 11–3 shows the …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-2 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx488 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

mott6:es-0023 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0023" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p488/b7" ;
    ev:boundingBox "308.98,217.85,551.87,397.9" ;
    ev:extractedText "Taper keys [Figure 11–3(a) and (b)] are designed to be inserted from the end of the shaft after the hub is in position rather than installing the key first and then sliding the hub over the key as with parallel keys. The taper extends over at least the length of the hub, and the height, H, measured at the end of the hub, is the same as for the parallel key. The taper is typically 1/8 in per foot. Note that this design gives a smaller bearing area on the sides of the key, and …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-2 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx488 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

mott6:es-0026 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0026" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p492/b2" ;
    ev:boundingBox "50.98,70.94,293.85,310.98" ;
    ev:extractedText "Keys are made from plain carbon steel, alloy steels, stainless steels, and some nonferrous metals. Even plastics are used for small devices under low loads. This book will focus primarily on steels for general industrial applications. Table 11–4 lists a variety of steels and one aluminum alloy to illustrate the types of materials available. (See Internet site 20.) For problem solutions in this book, it is recommended that the low-carbon steel SAE 1018 be considered for most …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-3 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx492 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

mott6:es-0032 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0032" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p493/b1" ;
    ev:boundingBox "62.98,282.06,305.79,366.13" ;
    ev:extractedText "The failure in bearing is related to the compressive stress on the side of the key, the side of the shaft keyseat, or the side of the hub keyseat. The area in compression is the same for either of these zones, L * (H/2). Thus, the failure occurs on the surface with the lowest compressive yield strength. Let’s define a design stress for compression as"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-4 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0057 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx493 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "partial-glyph-loss" .

mott6:es-0033 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0033" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p493/b21" ;
    ev:boundingBox "336.98,353.81,547.25,395.11" ;
    ev:extractedText "2. Select the size of the key from Table 11–1. 3. Specify a suitable design factor, N. In typical industrial applications, N = 3 is adequate to accommodate accidental overloads and shock."@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-4 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0052 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx493 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "partial-glyph-loss" .

mott6:es-0034 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0034" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p493/b25" ;
    ev:boundingBox "336.98,483.81,547.33,593.11" ;
    ev:extractedText "7. If a rectangular key is used, or if either the shaft or the hub has a lower strength than the key, use Equation (11–4) to compute the minimum required length of the key based on bearing stress. Also, use Equation (11–2) or (11–5) to compute the minimum required length based on shear of the key. The larger of the two computed lengths governs the design. Check to be sure that the computed length is shorter than the hub length. If not, a higher-strength material must be …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-4 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx493 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

mott6:es-0035 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0035" ;
    mdcore:analystNote "Primary Mott evidence that spline and multiple keys are alternatives to a single key."@en ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p493/b25" ;
    ev:boundingBox "336.98,483.81,547.33,593.11" ;
    ev:extractedText "7. If a rectangular key is used, or if either the shaft or the hub has a lower strength than the key, use Equation (11–4) to compute the minimum required length of the key based on bearing stress. Also, use Equation (11–2) or (11–5) to compute the minimum required length based on shear of the key. The larger of the two computed lengths governs the design. Check to be sure that the computed length is shorter than the hub length. If not, a higher-strength material must be …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-4 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx493 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

mott6:es-0049 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0049" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p496/b1" ;
    ev:boundingBox "50.98,234.68,293.9,390.73" ;
    ev:extractedText "parameters of D (major diameter), d (minor diameter), W (spline width), and h (spline depth). The dimensions for d, W, and h are related to the nominal major diameter D by the formulas given in Table 11–5. Note that the values of h and d differ according to the use of the spline. The permanent fit, A, is used when the mating part is not to be moved after installation. The B fit is used if the mating part will be moved along the shaft without a torque load. When the mating …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-5 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx496 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

mott6:es-0063 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0063" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p499/b16" ;
    ev:boundingBox "62.98,259.36,305.81,307.43" ;
    ev:extractedText "Sometimes the diameter of the pin is purposely made small to ensure that the pin will break if a moderate overload is encountered, in order to protect critical parts of a mechanism. Such a pin is called a shear pin."@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-6 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx499 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

mott6:es-0070 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0070" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p587/b1" ;
    ev:boundingBox "62.98,51.85,303.17,75.9" ;
    ev:extractedText "relationship between load, P, and life, L, for rolling contact bearings can be stated as"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n14 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n14-7 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx587 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

mott6:es-0071 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0071" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p587/b7" ;
    ev:boundingBox "62.98,125.85,208.79,139.39" ;
    ev:extractedText "where k = 3.00 for ball bearings"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n14 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n14-7 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0606 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx587 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "partial-glyph-loss" .

mott6:es-0075 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0075" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p591/b14" ;
    ev:boundingBox "320.97,285.78,563.86,393.82" ;
    ev:extractedText "Most people do not think in terms of the number of revolutions that a shaft makes. Rather, they consider the speed of rotation of the shaft, usually in rpm, and the design life of the machine, usually in hours of operation. The design life is specified by the designer, considering the application. As a guide, Table 14–4 can be used. Now, for a specified design life in hours, h, and a known speed of rotation in rpm, the number of design revolutions for the bearing would be"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n14 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n14-8 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx591 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

